<section class="container container--lg my-10">
  <h2 class="h1 mb-3">Migration, not transliteration</h2>
  <p class="medium">
    Automated C-to-Rust conversion tends to fall into the trap of just producing Rust that compiles
    but not Rust that's safer: it preserves the aliasing, lifetimes, and manual memory management of
    the original inside <code>unsafe</code> blocks, leaving you with the same bugs in a language you
    now also have to learn. We treat the migration as a redesign instead. A pointer and a length
    become a slice; a global with a documented locking convention becomes a type that makes the
    convention unrepresentable if you get it wrong; an error code half the callers ignored becomes a
    <code>Result</code> that warns callers when left unused.
  </p>
  <p class="medium mt-2">
    The result is a codebase that's better than what you started with, not a translation of it. That
    takes longer than running a converter. It's also the only version of this work that pays back.
  </p>
</section>

{%
  set steps = [
